// Class 9 Science practice bank, part 1: Matter in Our Surroundings to Tissues (Chemistry and Biology).
StudyData.addPractice(9, "science", {
  "matter-in-our-surroundings": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which state of matter is the most compressible?", options: ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "Solid and liquid equally"], answer: 2, explain: "Gas particles have very large spaces between them, so they can be pushed close together easily.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Solid carbon dioxide is commonly called:", options: ["Quicklime", "Dry ice", "Naphthalene", "Soda ice"], answer: 1, explain: "Solid CO₂ is dry ice; at 1 atmosphere it changes directly into gas without melting.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of pressure is the:", options: ["atmosphere", "bar", "kelvin", "pascal"], answer: 3, explain: "Pressure is measured in pascal (Pa). 1 atm = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.", level: 1 },
      { q: "0 °C is equal to ___ on the kelvin scale (nearest whole number).", options: ["273 K", "0 K", "373 K", "−273 K"], answer: 0, explain: "T(K) = T(°C) + 273 = 0 + 273 = 273 K.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The direct change of a gas into a solid on cooling, without passing through the liquid state, is called:", options: ["condensation", "freezing", "deposition", "sublimation"], answer: 2, explain: "Vapour changing directly to solid is deposition; solid to vapour is sublimation.", level: 1 },

      { q: "50 °C on the kelvin scale is:", options: ["223 K", "323 K", "373 K", "50 K"], answer: 1, explain: "T(K) = 50 + 273 = 323 K. Subtracting 273 gives the wrong value 223 K.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The temperature of boiling water stays at 100 °C even when it is heated continuously.\nReason (R): The heat supplied is used up as latent heat of vaporisation to overcome the forces of attraction between particles.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "At the boiling point the heat goes into changing the state (latent heat), not into raising the temperature.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Ravi spreads a wet shirt on a terrace on a hot, dry, windy afternoon. Meena folds an identical wet shirt and keeps it in a damp, closed bathroom. Which statement is correct?", options: ["Meena's shirt dries first because folding keeps heat in", "Ravi's shirt dries first because larger surface area, higher temperature, wind and low humidity all speed up evaporation", "Both dry at the same rate because the cloth is the same", "Meena's shirt dries first because humid air helps evaporation"], answer: 1, explain: "Evaporation increases with surface area, temperature and wind speed, and decreases with humidity. Ravi's conditions favour all four.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Water in an earthen pot (matka) feels colder on a hot, dry day than on a humid day because:", options: ["clay absorbs cold from the air", "water particles move more slowly in dry air", "water seeping through the pores evaporates faster in dry air and takes heat from the water inside", "dry air contains more water vapour to cool the pot"], answer: 2, explain: "Faster evaporation from the pot's surface takes more heat (latent heat) from the water inside, cooling it more.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these will NOT increase the rate of evaporation of water from an open dish?", options: ["Pouring the water into a wider dish", "Placing the dish in a breeze", "Warming the dish", "Putting the dish in a closed, humid room"], answer: 3, explain: "High humidity slows evaporation because the air already holds a lot of water vapour.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Evaporation takes place only at the boiling point of a liquid.\nReason (R): Evaporation is a surface phenomenon.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Evaporation occurs at any temperature below the boiling point, so A is false. It is indeed a surface phenomenon, so R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A solid has a melting point of 320 K. At 45 °C and 1 atm the substance will be:", options: ["liquid, because 45 °C is greater than 0 °C", "liquid, because 318 K is close to 320 K", "solid, because 45 °C is 318 K, which is below 320 K", "gas, because 45 °C is above room temperature"], answer: 2, explain: "45 + 273 = 318 K, which is lower than the melting point 320 K, so the substance has not yet melted.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A drop of acetone and a drop of water at the same room temperature are placed on your palm. Acetone feels colder because it:", options: ["evaporates faster and takes more heat from the palm", "has a higher boiling point than water", "was stored at a lower temperature", "condenses on the palm and releases cold"], answer: 0, explain: "Acetone evaporates much faster, so it absorbs more heat per second from the palm, producing a stronger cooling effect.", level: 3 },
      { q: "While a solid is melting at its melting point and is heated continuously, which statement is correct?", options: ["The average kinetic energy of the particles rises steadily", "The heat absorbed is used to overcome the forces of attraction between particles, so the temperature stays constant", "The heat absorbed is lost to the surroundings", "The particles stop moving"], answer: 1, explain: "This hidden heat is the latent heat of fusion; it changes the state without raising the temperature.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define sublimation and give one example.", a: "Sublimation is the direct change of a solid into vapour on heating, without passing through the liquid state. Example: camphor (also naphthalene or ammonium chloride).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why can a large quantity of LPG be stored in a small cylinder?", a: "Gas particles have large spaces between them, so a gas is highly compressible. Under high pressure it can be compressed (and liquefied) into a small volume.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why do we wear cotton clothes in summer?", a: "Cotton is a good absorber of water, so it soaks up sweat and exposes it to the air over a large surface.\nThe sweat evaporates quickly, taking heat (latent heat of vaporisation) from the body, which cools us.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write two differences between evaporation and boiling.", a: "(1) Evaporation takes place at any temperature below the boiling point; boiling takes place only at a fixed temperature, the boiling point.\n(2) Evaporation is a surface phenomenon; boiling is a bulk phenomenon in which bubbles form throughout the liquid.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name three factors that affect the rate of evaporation and explain the effect of each.", a: "(1) Surface area: a larger exposed surface lets more particles escape, so evaporation is faster.\n(2) Temperature: at higher temperature more particles have enough kinetic energy to leave, so evaporation is faster.\n(3) Humidity (or wind speed): in humid air the air already holds much water vapour, so evaporation is slower; moving air carries vapour away and speeds up evaporation.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using the particle model, explain why solids are rigid, liquids can flow and gases fill the whole container.", a: "Solids: particles are held by strong forces of attraction in fixed positions and can only vibrate, so the solid keeps a definite shape and volume.\nLiquids: forces are weaker and particles can slide past each other, so liquids flow and take the shape of the container but keep a fixed volume.\nGases: forces of attraction are very weak and particles move randomly at high speed, so they spread to fill the entire container.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Convert (a) 37 °C into kelvin, (b) 400 K into degree Celsius and (c) −10 °C into kelvin.", a: "Use T(K) = T(°C) + 273.\n(a) 37 + 273 = 310 K.\n(b) T(°C) = 400 − 273 = 127 °C.\n(c) −10 + 273 = 263 K.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Ice at 263 K is heated steadily until it is all converted into steam. Describe the changes in temperature and state at each stage.", a: "1. Ice warms from 263 K (−10 °C) up to 273 K (0 °C); the temperature rises.\n2. At 273 K the ice melts; the temperature stays constant because the heat is used as latent heat of fusion.\n3. The water formed warms from 273 K to 373 K (100 °C); temperature rises.\n4. At 373 K the water boils; the temperature stays constant as the heat is used as latent heat of vaporisation.\n5. When all the water has become steam, further heating raises the temperature of the steam above 373 K.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe an activity to show that the particles of matter are extremely small.", a: "1. Take 2–3 crystals of potassium permanganate and dissolve them in 100 mL of water; the water turns deep purple.\n2. Take 10 mL of this solution and add it to 90 mL of clear water in another beaker.\n3. Repeat this dilution 4–5 times, each time taking 10 mL of the previous solution and adding it to fresh water.\n4. Even after many dilutions the water is still faintly coloured.\n5. This shows that a few crystals contain a huge number of tiny particles that spread through the water, so the particles of matter must be extremely small.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A student heated 100 g of a solid substance and noted its temperature every minute. The readings from minute 0 to minute 8 were: 30, 40, 50, 60, 60, 60, 60, 72, 85 (in °C). The substance was fully solid at minute 3 and fully liquid at minute 7.\n(i) What is the melting point of the substance in kelvin?\n(ii) Why did the temperature not rise from minute 3 to minute 6?\n(iii) Name the heat energy absorbed during this period.", a: "(i) The temperature stayed constant at 60 °C, so melting point = 60 + 273 = 333 K.\n(ii) The heat supplied was used to overcome the forces of attraction between the particles and change the solid into liquid, not to raise the temperature.\n(iii) It is the latent heat of fusion.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "is-matter-around-us-pure": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following is a pure substance?", options: ["Sea water", "Air", "Distilled water", "Brass"], answer: 2, explain: "Distilled water is a compound (H₂O) with particles of one kind only. The others are mixtures.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Air is best described as a:", options: ["compound", "homogeneous mixture", "heterogeneous mixture", "pure element"], answer: 1, explain: "Air is a uniform mixture of gases (nitrogen, oxygen and others) in no fixed proportion.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following mixtures does NOT scatter a beam of light?", options: ["Milk", "Fog", "Starch solution", "Sugar solution"], answer: 3, explain: "A true solution has particles smaller than 1 nm, too small to scatter light. Milk, fog and starch solution are colloids.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The only metal that is liquid at room temperature is:", options: ["bromine", "mercury", "iodine", "sodium"], answer: 1, explain: "Mercury is the liquid metal. Bromine is a liquid non-metal.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Shaving cream is a colloid of the type:", options: ["emulsion", "gel", "foam", "aerosol"], answer: 2, explain: "Shaving cream has a gas dispersed in a liquid, which is a foam.", level: 1 },

      { q: "15 g of common salt is dissolved in 135 g of water. The concentration of the solution (mass by mass percentage) is:", options: ["11.1%", "10%", "15%", "9%"], answer: 1, explain: "Mass of solution = 15 + 135 = 150 g. Percentage = (15 ÷ 150) × 100 = 10%.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A mixture of iron filings and sulphur powder is heated strongly and a black solid is formed which is NOT attracted by a magnet. This is because:", options: ["the sulphur has covered the iron completely", "a new compound, iron sulphide, with properties different from iron has formed", "the heating has destroyed the iron", "the mixture has become a solution"], answer: 1, explain: "Strong heating gives the compound FeS, whose properties differ from those of iron and sulphur.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Sugar solution shows the Tyndall effect.\nReason (R): The particles of a true solution are smaller than 1 nm.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Particles smaller than 1 nm cannot scatter light, so a true solution does not show the Tyndall effect. A is false and R is true.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Rohan has three beakers: muddy water, salt solution and milk. A torch beam is passed through each. The beam is clearly visible in two beakers, but only one of them keeps its particles evenly spread after hours and passes through filter paper. That beaker is:", options: ["muddy water", "salt solution", "milk", "none of them"], answer: 2, explain: "Muddy water (suspension) settles and is filtered. Milk (colloid) scatters light, does not settle and passes through filter paper. Salt solution does not scatter light.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Sarita dissolves 30 g of sugar in 120 g of water to make a sherbet base. The mass by mass percentage of sugar is:", options: ["25%", "20%", "30%", "75%"], answer: 1, explain: "Mass of solution = 30 + 120 = 150 g. Percentage = (30 ÷ 150) × 100 = 20%. Using 120 g in the denominator gives the wrong value 25%.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A mixture of iron filings and sulphur can be separated using a magnet.\nReason (R): In a mixture, the components keep their own properties.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Iron keeps its magnetic property in the mixture, so a magnet pulls it out. R explains A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A solution has 20 g of sugar in 80 g of water. After 100 g more water is added, the concentration (mass by mass) becomes:", options: ["20%", "12.5%", "10%", "5%"], answer: 2, explain: "Mass of sugar stays 20 g. New mass of solution = 20 + 180 = 200 g. Percentage = (20 ÷ 200) × 100 = 10%.", level: 3 },
      { q: "To prepare 500 g of a 4% (mass by mass) glucose solution, the mass of water needed is:", options: ["20 g", "400 g", "496 g", "480 g"], answer: 3, explain: "Glucose = 4% of 500 = 20 g. Water = 500 − 20 = 480 g.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following colloid–type pairs is INCORRECT?", options: ["Fog – aerosol", "Shaving cream – foam", "Milk of magnesia – gel", "Smoke – aerosol"], answer: 2, explain: "Milk of magnesia is a sol (solid in liquid), not a gel. A gel is a liquid dispersed in a solid, such as jelly.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is the Tyndall effect?", a: "It is the scattering of a beam of light by the particles of a colloid (or suspension), which makes the path of the beam visible.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium in fog.", a: "The dispersed phase is liquid (tiny water droplets) and the dispersion medium is gas (air).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write two differences between a true solution and a suspension.", a: "(1) A true solution is homogeneous and its particles (smaller than 1 nm) cannot be seen; a suspension is heterogeneous and its particles are visible.\n(2) In a solution the particles do not settle and do not scatter light; in a suspension they settle on standing and scatter light.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between a physical change and a chemical change with one example each.", a: "In a physical change no new substance is formed and only physical properties change; example: melting of ice.\nIn a chemical change a new substance with different properties is formed; example: rusting of iron (or burning of paper).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Classify each as element, compound or mixture: sodium chloride, air, copper, sea water, water, brass.", a: "Elements: copper.\nCompounds: sodium chloride, water.\nMixtures: air, sea water, brass (an alloy, a homogeneous mixture).\n(Any correct classification of all six earns 3 marks.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A solution contains 25 g of solute in 500 mL of solution. A second solution contains 9 g of solute in 90 g of water. Find the concentration of each: the first as mass by volume percentage and the second as mass by mass percentage.", a: "First: mass by volume % = (mass of solute ÷ volume of solution) × 100 = (25 ÷ 500) × 100 = 5%.\nSecond: mass of solution = 9 + 90 = 99 g.\nMass by mass % = (9 ÷ 99) × 100 = 9.09% (about 9.1%).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Iron filings and sulphur powder are (a) mixed and (b) mixed and then heated strongly. Give three differences in what you observe.", a: "(1) The mixture can be separated by a magnet, which attracts the iron; the heated product (iron sulphide) is not attracted.\n(2) In the mixture the colours of iron and sulphur are still seen; the product is a black solid that looks different.\n(3) The mixture can have any proportion of iron and sulphur and keeps their properties; the compound has a fixed composition and entirely new properties.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Differentiate between a solution, a colloid and a suspension on the basis of (i) type of mixture, (ii) particle size and visibility, (iii) settling on standing, (iv) filtration, (v) Tyndall effect.", a: "Solution: (i) homogeneous; (ii) smaller than 1 nm, not visible; (iii) does not settle; (iv) cannot be filtered out; (v) no Tyndall effect.\nColloid: (i) heterogeneous; (ii) too small to be seen individually but big enough to scatter light; (iii) does not settle; (iv) cannot be filtered with ordinary filter paper but can be separated by centrifugation; (v) shows the Tyndall effect.\nSuspension: (i) heterogeneous; (ii) particles are large and visible; (iii) settle on standing; (iv) can be filtered; (v) scatters light (path of beam visible).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "At a certain temperature a saturated solution of a salt has 25 g of salt dissolved in 75 g of water. Calculate (a) the mass by mass percentage of the saturated solution, (b) the mass of salt present in 200 g of this saturated solution, (c) the mass of water in that 200 g, and (d) state what 'saturated' means.", a: "(a) Mass of solution = 25 + 75 = 100 g. Percentage = (25 ÷ 100) × 100 = 25%.\n(b) Salt in 200 g = 25% of 200 = 50 g.\n(c) Water = 200 − 50 = 150 g.\n(d) A saturated solution cannot dissolve any more solute at that temperature; the amount of solute in it is the solubility.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: In an early morning in a forest, sunlight enters through gaps in the trees and the straight paths of the rays can be seen clearly through the mist. The same sun rays passing through a glass of clear salt solution kept on a table in the room do not show any visible path.\n(i) Name the phenomenon that makes the path of the rays visible in the mist.\n(ii) Why is the path of light not visible through the salt solution?\n(iii) Mist consists of tiny water droplets in air. Name the type of colloid it is.", a: "(i) The Tyndall effect: the colloidal droplets in the mist scatter the light.\n(ii) The particles of a true solution are smaller than 1 nm, too small to scatter light.\n(iii) It is an aerosol (liquid dispersed in a gas).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "atoms-and-molecules": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The law of conservation of mass was given by:", options: ["Proust", "Lavoisier", "Dalton", "Avogadro"], answer: 1, explain: "Lavoisier stated that mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. Proust gave the law of constant proportions.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The chemical symbol of potassium is:", options: ["P", "Po", "K", "Pt"], answer: 2, explain: "K comes from its Latin name kalium. P is phosphorus.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Positively charged ions are called:", options: ["cations", "anions", "molecules", "radicals only"], answer: 0, explain: "Cations carry a positive charge (e.g. Na⁺, Mg²⁺); anions are negative.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The atomicity of a phosphorus molecule (P₄) is:", options: ["1", "2", "8", "4"], answer: 3, explain: "One P₄ molecule has four phosphorus atoms.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The value of the Avogadro constant is:", options: ["6.022 × 10²²", "6.022 × 10²³", "6.022 × 10²⁴", "3.011 × 10²³"], answer: 1, explain: "One mole of any species contains 6.022 × 10²³ particles.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The formula unit mass of sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃, is: (Na = 23 u, C = 12 u, O = 16 u)", options: ["82 u", "106 u", "60 u", "128 u"], answer: 1, explain: "2 × 23 + 12 + 3 × 16 = 46 + 12 + 48 = 106 u.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The number of moles in 54 g of water is: (H = 1 u, O = 16 u)", options: ["2", "6", "3", "1.5"], answer: 2, explain: "Molar mass of H₂O = 18 g. n = 54 ÷ 18 = 3 mol.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The correct formula of ammonium sulphate is:", options: ["NH₄SO₄", "(NH₄)₂SO₄", "NH₄(SO₄)₂", "(NH₃)₂SO₄"], answer: 1, explain: "NH₄⁺ has charge +1 and SO₄²⁻ has −2, so two ammonium ions are needed: (NH₄)₂SO₄.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In pure water from any source, hydrogen and oxygen are always present in the mass ratio 1 : 8.\nReason (R): A chemical compound always contains its elements in a fixed proportion by mass.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "This is the law of constant proportions, which explains why water always has H : O = 1 : 8.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "In an experiment 2.4 g of magnesium ribbon was burnt completely in air and 4.0 g of white magnesium oxide was obtained. The mass of oxygen that combined with the magnesium, and the Mg : O mass ratio, are:", options: ["2.4 g and 1 : 1", "1.6 g and 3 : 2", "6.4 g and 3 : 8", "1.6 g and 2 : 3"], answer: 1, explain: "Oxygen = 4.0 − 2.4 = 1.6 g (law of conservation of mass). Ratio Mg : O = 2.4 : 1.6 = 3 : 2.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): 1 mole of oxygen molecules and 1 mole of hydrogen molecules have the same mass.\nReason (R): One mole of any substance contains the same number of particles.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false: 1 mol O₂ = 32 g while 1 mol H₂ = 2 g. R is true: each mole holds 6.022 × 10²³ particles.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A pinch of table salt weighs 0.585 g. The number of formula units of NaCl in it is: (Na = 23 u, Cl = 35.5 u)", options: ["6.022 × 10²³", "3.011 × 10²¹", "6.022 × 10²¹", "6.022 × 10²⁰"], answer: 2, explain: "Molar mass of NaCl = 58.5 g. n = 0.585 ÷ 58.5 = 0.01 mol. N = 0.01 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 6.022 × 10²¹.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The mass of 3.011 × 10²³ molecules of oxygen (O₂) is: (O = 16 u)", options: ["32 g", "8 g", "16 g", "64 g"], answer: 2, explain: "3.011 × 10²³ is half a mole. Mass = 0.5 × 32 = 16 g.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following samples contains the largest number of molecules? (H = 1 u, C = 12 u, N = 14 u, O = 16 u)", options: ["16 g of O₂", "28 g of N₂", "22 g of CO₂", "4 g of H₂"], answer: 3, explain: "Moles: O₂ = 16 ÷ 32 = 0.5; N₂ = 28 ÷ 28 = 1; CO₂ = 22 ÷ 44 = 0.5; H₂ = 4 ÷ 2 = 2. H₂ has the most moles, so the most molecules.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the law of constant proportions.", a: "In a chemical substance the elements are always present in definite proportions by mass, whatever the source of the substance.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the atomic mass unit (u)?", a: "One atomic mass unit is 1/12th of the mass of one atom of carbon-12.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "3.0 g of carbon burns completely in 8.0 g of oxygen to form 11.0 g of carbon dioxide. What mass of carbon dioxide will be formed when 3.0 g of carbon is burnt in 50.0 g of oxygen? Which law is illustrated?", a: "3.0 g of carbon combines with only 8.0 g of oxygen, whatever more oxygen is present; the excess oxygen is left unused.\nSo 11.0 g of carbon dioxide is formed.\nThis illustrates the law of constant proportions.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Define atomicity. Give the atomicity of ozone and of sulphur (S₈).", a: "Atomicity is the number of atoms present in one molecule of an element.\nOzone (O₃) has atomicity 3 (triatomic) and sulphur (S₈) has atomicity 8 (poly-atomic).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write the chemical formulae of (a) potassium sulphate, (b) magnesium nitrate, (c) aluminium chloride. Show the criss-cross of valencies.", a: "(a) K⁺ and SO₄²⁻: criss-cross gives K₂SO₄.\n(b) Mg²⁺ and NO₃⁻: criss-cross gives Mg(NO₃)₂ (bracket needed for the polyatomic ion).\n(c) Al³⁺ and Cl⁻: criss-cross gives AlCl₃.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Calculate the molecular or formula unit mass of (a) glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, (b) calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂ and (c) ammonia, NH₃. (H = 1 u, C = 12 u, N = 14 u, O = 16 u, Ca = 40 u)", a: "(a) C₆H₁₂O₆ = 6 × 12 + 12 × 1 + 6 × 16 = 72 + 12 + 96 = 180 u.\n(b) Ca(OH)₂ = 40 + 2 × (16 + 1) = 40 + 34 = 74 u.\n(c) NH₃ = 14 + 3 × 1 = 17 u.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Calculate the number of moles and the number of molecules in 4.9 g of sulphuric acid, H₂SO₄. (H = 1 u, S = 32 u, O = 16 u; N₀ = 6.022 × 10²³)", a: "Molar mass of H₂SO₄ = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g.\nNumber of moles n = m ÷ M = 4.9 ÷ 98 = 0.05 mol.\nNumber of molecules N = n × N₀ = 0.05 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.011 × 10²² molecules.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State the main postulates of Dalton's atomic theory.", a: "1. All matter is made of very small particles called atoms.\n2. Atoms can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.\n3. All atoms of a given element are identical in mass and chemical properties.\n4. Atoms of different elements have different masses and chemical properties.\n5. Atoms combine in small whole-number ratios to form compounds, and the relative number and kinds of atoms in a given compound are constant.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "22 g of carbon dioxide are taken. Calculate (a) the number of moles, (b) the number of molecules, (c) the total number of atoms and (d) the mass of one molecule. (C = 12 u, O = 16 u; N₀ = 6.022 × 10²³)", a: "Molar mass of CO₂ = 12 + 32 = 44 g.\n(a) n = 22 ÷ 44 = 0.5 mol.\n(b) N = 0.5 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.011 × 10²³ molecules.\n(c) Each molecule has 3 atoms, so atoms = 3 × 3.011 × 10²³ = 9.033 × 10²³.\n(d) Mass of one molecule = 44 ÷ (6.022 × 10²³) = 7.31 × 10⁻²³ g.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Neha places a beaker with baking soda and vinegar on a balance in an open room. After mixing, bubbles form and the balance reading falls from 150.0 g to 148.9 g. She repeats the experiment in a closed bottle with a balloon on its mouth and finds that the total mass does not change.\n(i) Why did the mass decrease in the open beaker?\n(ii) Which law is verified by the closed-bottle experiment?\n(iii) What mass of gas escaped in the open beaker?", a: "(i) Carbon dioxide gas formed in the reaction escaped into the air, so the mass left on the balance was less.\n(ii) The law of conservation of mass: total mass of reactants equals total mass of products.\n(iii) Mass of gas escaped = 150.0 − 148.9 = 1.1 g.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "structure-of-the-atom": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The electron was discovered by:", options: ["J.J. Thomson", "E. Goldstein", "J. Chadwick", "Ernest Rutherford"], answer: 0, explain: "J.J. Thomson identified the electron. Goldstein found canal rays and Chadwick found the neutron.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The charge on a neutron is:", options: ["+1", "−1", "zero", "+2"], answer: 2, explain: "A neutron is electrically neutral; its mass is nearly equal to that of a proton.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The atomic number of an element is equal to the number of:", options: ["neutrons", "nucleons", "electrons in the outer shell", "protons"], answer: 3, explain: "Atomic number Z = number of protons in the nucleus; it identifies the element.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in the L shell is:", options: ["2", "8", "18", "32"], answer: 1, explain: "L is the second shell: 2n² = 2 × 2² = 8.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Thomson's model of the atom is compared to:", options: ["a watermelon with seeds in a red pulp", "a hard solid ball", "the solar system", "a hollow sphere"], answer: 0, explain: "Positive charge is spread like the pulp and electrons are embedded like seeds.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The number of neutrons in an atom of ³⁷₁₇Cl is:", options: ["17", "37", "20", "54"], answer: 2, explain: "Neutrons = A − Z = 37 − 17 = 20.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which pair represents isotopes?", options: ["¹⁴₆C and ¹⁴₇N", "¹²₆C and ¹⁴₆C", "⁴⁰₂₀Ca and ⁴⁰₁₈Ar", "¹₁H and ¹⁶₈O"], answer: 1, explain: "Isotopes have the same atomic number (6) but different mass numbers (12 and 14). The pairs in the first and third options are isobars.", level: 2 },
      { q: "An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 8, 2. It is:", options: ["argon", "magnesium", "potassium", "calcium"], answer: 3, explain: "Total electrons = 2 + 8 + 8 + 2 = 20, so Z = 20, which is calcium.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties.\nReason (R): Isotopes of an element have different mass numbers.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true, but chemical properties depend on the number of electrons (same for isotopes), not on mass number.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "An element Y has atomic number 15 and mass number 31. Which statement about it is correct?", options: ["It has 16 neutrons and valency 3", "It has 15 neutrons and valency 5", "It has 31 neutrons and valency 3", "It has 16 neutrons and valency 5"], answer: 0, explain: "Neutrons = 31 − 15 = 16. Configuration is 2, 8, 5, so valency = 8 − 5 = 3.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): All atoms of the same element must have the same number of neutrons.\nReason (R): All atoms of the same element have the same number of protons.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Atoms of an element can have different numbers of neutrons (isotopes), so A is false. The number of protons is fixed, so R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A hospital uses radioactive cobalt-60 (⁶⁰₂₇Co) to treat cancer. Ordinary cobalt is ⁵⁹₂₇Co. These two atoms differ in their number of:", options: ["protons", "electrons", "neutrons", "valence electrons"], answer: 2, explain: "Both have 27 protons and 27 electrons; they are isotopes with 33 and 32 neutrons respectively.", level: 3 },
      { q: "An element has two isotopes of mass numbers 10 and 11 in the ratio 20% and 80%. Its average atomic mass is:", options: ["10.5 u", "10.2 u", "10.8 u", "11.0 u"], answer: 2, explain: "(10 × 20 + 11 × 80) ÷ 100 = (200 + 880) ÷ 100 = 10.8 u.", level: 3 },
      { q: "An ion X²⁻ has 10 electrons and the atom has mass number 18. The number of neutrons in the nucleus is:", options: ["8", "10", "18", "12"], answer: 1, explain: "The ion gained 2 electrons, so the atom had 8 electrons and 8 protons. Neutrons = 18 − 8 = 10.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the sub-atomic particle discovered through canal rays and state its charge.", a: "The proton, discovered by E. Goldstein; it carries a positive charge (+1).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "An atom has 12 protons and 12 neutrons. Write its atomic number and mass number.", a: "Atomic number = number of protons = 12.\nMass number = protons + neutrons = 12 + 12 = 24.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Differentiate between isotopes and isobars with one example of each.", a: "Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, e.g. ¹²₆C and ¹⁴₆C.\nIsobars are atoms of different elements with the same mass number but different atomic numbers, e.g. ⁴⁰₂₀Ca and ⁴⁰₁₈Ar.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Argon (Z = 18) is chemically inert but sodium (Z = 11) is highly reactive. Explain using electronic configurations.", a: "Argon is 2, 8, 8: its outermost shell has 8 electrons (a complete octet), so it has no tendency to lose, gain or share electrons.\nSodium is 2, 8, 1: it has one valence electron which it easily loses to attain a stable octet, so it is reactive.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State three differences between Thomson's model and Rutherford's model of the atom.", a: "(1) Thomson: positive charge is spread over the whole atom; Rutherford: positive charge is concentrated in a tiny nucleus.\n(2) Thomson: electrons are embedded in the positive sphere; Rutherford: electrons revolve around the nucleus.\n(3) Thomson: the atom has no empty space; Rutherford: most of the atom is empty space.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Find the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in (a) ²³₁₁Na, (b) ²⁷₁₃Al, (c) ³²₁₆S.", a: "(a) ²³₁₁Na: protons 11, neutrons 23 − 11 = 12, electrons 11.\n(b) ²⁷₁₃Al: protons 13, neutrons 27 − 13 = 14, electrons 13.\n(c) ³²₁₆S: protons 16, neutrons 32 − 16 = 16, electrons 16.\n(In a neutral atom electrons = protons.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State the three main postulates of Bohr's model of the atom.", a: "(1) Electrons revolve only in certain special orbits, called discrete orbits or energy levels (shells).\n(2) While revolving in these orbits electrons do not radiate energy, so the atom is stable.\n(3) The shells are named K, L, M, N… or n = 1, 2, 3, 4… counting from the nucleus, and each shell can hold a maximum of 2n² electrons.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe Rutherford's α-particle scattering experiment, its observations and conclusions.", a: "Set-up: fast-moving α-particles were fired at a very thin gold foil and their paths were observed.\nObservations: (1) most α-particles passed straight through; (2) some were deflected by small angles; (3) very few (about 1 in 12,000) bounced back.\nConclusions: (1) most of the atom is empty space; (2) all the positive charge and almost all the mass is concentrated in a tiny nucleus; (3) electrons revolve around the nucleus.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Copper has two isotopes ⁶³Cu and ⁶⁵Cu and its average atomic mass is 63.5 u. Find the percentage of each isotope in a sample.", a: "Let the percentage of ⁶³Cu be x; then ⁶⁵Cu is (100 − x).\nAverage mass = [63x + 65(100 − x)] ÷ 100 = 63.5\n63x + 6500 − 65x = 6350\n−2x = −150, so x = 75.\n⁶³Cu = 75% and ⁶⁵Cu = 25%.\nCheck: (63 × 75 + 65 × 25) ÷ 100 = 63.5 u.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Three atoms have these values: P (atomic number 6, mass number 12), Q (atomic number 6, mass number 14) and R (atomic number 7, mass number 14).\n(i) Which two atoms are isotopes of each other?\n(ii) Which two atoms are isobars?\n(iii) Write the electronic configuration of R and find its valency.", a: "(i) P and Q are isotopes (same atomic number 6, different mass numbers).\n(ii) Q and R are isobars (same mass number 14, different atomic numbers).\n(iii) R has 7 electrons: configuration 2, 5. Valency = 8 − 5 = 3.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "fundamental-unit-of-life": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The cell theory was proposed by:", options: ["Robert Hooke and Leeuwenhoek", "Purkinje", "Schleiden and Schwann", "Robert Brown"], answer: 2, explain: "Schleiden (1838) and Schwann (1839) proposed that all plants and animals are made of cells; Virchow later added that cells arise from pre-existing cells.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The nucleus of the cell was discovered by:", options: ["Robert Hooke", "Leeuwenhoek", "Rudolf Virchow", "Robert Brown"], answer: 3, explain: "Robert Brown discovered the nucleus in 1831.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Rough endoplasmic reticulum appears rough because of the:", options: ["ribosomes attached to its surface", "lysosomes inside it", "plastids on it", "vacuoles around it"], answer: 0, explain: "Ribosomes on its surface give it a rough look, and it makes proteins.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The plastids that contain chlorophyll are called:", options: ["leucoplasts", "chloroplasts", "vacuoles", "nucleoids"], answer: 1, explain: "Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll for photosynthesis; leucoplasts are colourless and store food.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Gametes are formed by the type of cell division called:", options: ["mitosis", "budding", "meiosis", "binary fission"], answer: 2, explain: "Meiosis takes place in reproductive organs and gives four cells with half the number of chromosomes.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A dried raisin swells when soaked in water. The water around the raisin is ___ compared with the raisin's contents.", options: ["hypertonic", "isotonic", "saturated", "hypotonic"], answer: 3, explain: "Water moves by osmosis into the raisin from the hypotonic (more dilute) surrounding.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following is found in plant cells but NOT in animal cells?", options: ["Plastids", "Mitochondria", "Golgi apparatus", "Lysosomes"], answer: 0, explain: "Plant cells have a cell wall, plastids and a large central vacuole. Animal cells lack plastids.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which two cell organelles have their own DNA and ribosomes?", options: ["Golgi apparatus and lysosomes", "Mitochondria and plastids", "Rough ER and smooth ER", "Vacuoles and lysosomes"], answer: 1, explain: "Mitochondria and plastids contain their own DNA and ribosomes and can make some of their own proteins.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Mitosis produces two daughter cells identical to the parent cell.\nReason (R): Mitosis takes place in reproductive organs to form gametes.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: the daughter cells have the same chromosome number. R is false: gametes are formed by meiosis; mitosis is for growth and repair.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Asha places a thin peel of onion in 5% salt solution. Under the microscope the cell contents shrink away from the cell wall. The salt solution is ___ to the cell and the process seen is ___.", options: ["hypotonic; turgidity", "hypertonic; plasmolysis", "isotonic; osmosis", "hypertonic; endocytosis"], answer: 1, explain: "Water leaves the cell into the more concentrated (hypertonic) solution, so the cell contents shrink: plasmolysis.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Smooth ER is abundant in the liver cells of animals.\nReason (R): Smooth ER helps detoxify poisons and drugs.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The liver detoxifies many poisons and drugs, and smooth ER carries out this function, so R explains A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Heart muscle cells contain far more mitochondria than skin cells. This is because heart muscle cells:", options: ["have a cell wall", "need much more energy (ATP) for continuous contraction", "store more starch", "have no nucleus"], answer: 1, explain: "Mitochondria release energy as ATP. Heart cells contract throughout life, so they need many mitochondria.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two equal potato cubes are kept for an hour: cube A in solution P gains mass and cube B in solution Q loses mass. Which statement is correct?", options: ["P and Q are both hypertonic", "P and Q are both hypotonic", "P is hypertonic and Q is hypotonic", "P is hypotonic and Q is hypertonic"], answer: 3, explain: "Cube A gains water, so P is hypotonic to the cells. Cube B loses water, so Q is hypertonic.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A protein made in a cell is to be released from it. The correct route is:", options: ["Golgi apparatus → rough ER → vesicle", "rough ER → Golgi apparatus → vesicle for release", "smooth ER → lysosome → nucleus", "mitochondria → rough ER → Golgi apparatus"], answer: 1, explain: "Proteins are made on the rough ER, then modified and packaged into vesicles by the Golgi apparatus.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Who first named the 'cell' and what did he observe?", a: "Robert Hooke (1665) observed a thin slice of cork under a self-designed microscope and saw many box-like compartments, which he named cells.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the organelle that stores, modifies and packages the products of the cell in vesicles.", a: "The Golgi apparatus.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write two differences between mitosis and meiosis.", a: "(1) Mitosis gives two identical daughter cells with the same chromosome number; meiosis gives four cells, each with half the chromosome number.\n(2) Mitosis is for growth and repair of tissues; meiosis takes place in reproductive organs to form gametes.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is diffusion? Give two examples of its role in cells.", a: "Diffusion is the movement of a substance from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration.\nExamples: oxygen enters the cell and carbon dioxide leaves it by diffusion across the plasma membrane.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write three differences between a plant cell and an animal cell.", a: "(1) A plant cell has a rigid cell wall of cellulose outside the plasma membrane; an animal cell has no cell wall.\n(2) A plant cell has plastids (such as chloroplasts); an animal cell does not.\n(3) A mature plant cell has one large central vacuole; an animal cell has many small vacuoles.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the functions of the endoplasmic reticulum.", a: "(1) Rough ER has ribosomes on its surface and makes proteins.\n(2) Smooth ER makes lipids and, in liver cells, helps detoxify poisons and drugs.\n(3) ER helps in membrane biogenesis (making new membrane) and acts as a channel for transport of materials inside the cell.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the structure and function of mitochondria.", a: "(1) Mitochondria have a double membrane: a smooth outer membrane and a deeply folded inner membrane.\n(2) They have their own DNA and ribosomes, so they can make some of their own proteins.\n(3) They release energy in the form of ATP, so they are called the powerhouses of the cell.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is osmosis? Explain what happens to an animal cell and a plant cell when placed in (a) a hypotonic, (b) an isotonic and (c) a hypertonic solution.", a: "Osmosis is the movement of water through a selectively permeable membrane from a region of high water concentration to a region of low water concentration.\n(a) Hypotonic: both cells gain water. The animal cell swells and may burst; the plant cell becomes turgid but its cell wall prevents bursting.\n(b) Isotonic: no net movement of water, so there is no change in size.\n(c) Hypertonic: both cells lose water. The animal cell shrinks; in the plant cell the contents shrink away from the cell wall (plasmolysis).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe the structure of the nucleus and its functions. How does the nuclear region of a prokaryotic cell differ?", a: "1. The nucleus is enclosed by a double-layered nuclear membrane with pores, through which materials pass between nucleus and cytoplasm.\n2. It contains chromosomes made of DNA and protein; in a non-dividing cell the DNA is present as a network of thread-like chromatin.\n3. Functional segments of DNA are genes, which carry the information for inheritance of characters.\n4. The nucleus controls the activities of the cell and takes part in cell division.\n5. In a prokaryotic cell there is no nuclear membrane; the undefined nuclear region is called the nucleoid.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Meera soaks some dried raisins in plain water for two hours and finds that they become swollen and soft. She then puts the swollen raisins in a concentrated sugar syrup and finds that they shrink.\n(i) Why do the raisins swell in plain water?\n(ii) Why do they shrink in the sugar syrup?\n(iii) Which property of the plasma membrane allows this to happen?", a: "(i) Water enters the raisin cells by osmosis because the surrounding water is hypotonic to the cell contents.\n(ii) The syrup is hypertonic, so water moves out of the raisin cells into the syrup and they shrink.\n(iii) The plasma membrane is selectively permeable: it allows water to pass but not the dissolved substances freely.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "tissues": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which feature is typical of cells of meristematic tissue?", options: ["Large vacuoles", "Thick lignified walls", "Dense cytoplasm and a prominent nucleus", "Dead cells with no cytoplasm"], answer: 2, explain: "Meristematic cells divide actively, so they have dense cytoplasm, thin cellulose walls, prominent nuclei and few or no vacuoles.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A ligament connects:", options: ["muscle to bone", "bone to bone", "skin to muscle", "nerve to muscle"], answer: 1, explain: "Ligaments join bone to bone; tendons join muscle to bone.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Muscles that work under our voluntary control are:", options: ["cardiac muscles", "muscles of the iris", "smooth muscles", "striated muscles"], answer: 3, explain: "Striated (skeletal) muscles are voluntary. Cardiac, smooth and iris muscles are involuntary.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Xylem conducts:", options: ["water and minerals", "food from the leaves", "hormones only", "oxygen"], answer: 0, explain: "Xylem carries water and minerals upward from roots to other parts; phloem carries food.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The dead cells of cork have walls containing a waxy substance called:", options: ["cellulose", "lignin", "suberin", "cutin"], answer: 2, explain: "Suberin makes cork impervious to gases and water. Cutin is found on the epidermis and lignin in sclerenchyma.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Intercalary meristem is found:", options: ["at the tips of roots", "at the base of leaves or internodes", "in the cambium of a stem", "in the epidermis"], answer: 1, explain: "It occurs near the nodes, at the base of leaves or internodes, in some plants.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Chlorenchyma is:", options: ["collenchyma containing chlorophyll", "sclerenchyma with lignin", "parenchyma containing chlorophyll", "cork with chlorophyll"], answer: 2, explain: "Parenchyma that contains chlorophyll and carries out photosynthesis is chlorenchyma.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Xylem transports materials only in the upward direction.\nReason (R): Phloem transports materials in both upward and downward directions.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true, but the property of phloem does not explain the property of xylem.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A sprinter feels pain behind his heel after a race. The doctor says the band joining his calf muscle to the heel bone is torn. This band is a:", options: ["tendon", "ligament", "cartilage", "adipose tissue"], answer: 0, explain: "A tendon connects muscle to bone; it is strong with limited flexibility.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Reema can raise her arm whenever she wants, but she cannot start or stop her heart at will. The muscles of her arm and heart are respectively:", options: ["smooth and cardiac", "cardiac and smooth", "cardiac and striated", "striated and cardiac"], answer: 3, explain: "Arm muscles are striated and voluntary; heart muscle is cardiac and involuntary.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Cardiac muscle cells are multinucleate.\nReason (R): Cardiac muscle cells are cylindrical and branched.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Cardiac muscle cells are uninucleate, so A is false. They are cylindrical and branched, so R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A ring of bark, including the phloem, is removed all around a young tree trunk. After some weeks, what is most likely to be seen?", options: ["The tree dries up at once because water cannot reach the leaves", "The part of the stem below the ring swells because of stored food", "The stem above the ring swells because food made in the leaves cannot move down past the cut", "No change, because phloem is present only in roots"], answer: 2, explain: "Phloem carries food from leaves to other parts. Once it is cut, food collects above the ring. Water still rises through the xylem inside.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The tip of a growing shoot is cut off. Which activity will be affected first?", options: ["Increase in girth of the stem", "Increase in length of that shoot", "Transport of water through xylem", "Storage of food in parenchyma"], answer: 1, explain: "Apical meristem at the tip increases the length of the stem. Girth depends on the lateral meristem.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following pairs of epithelium and location is INCORRECT?", options: ["Stratified squamous epithelium – inner lining of the respiratory tract", "Squamous epithelium – lining of blood vessels", "Cuboidal epithelium – kidney tubules", "Columnar epithelium – inner lining of the intestine"], answer: 0, explain: "The respiratory tract is lined by ciliated columnar epithelium. Stratified squamous epithelium forms the skin.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is differentiation?", a: "Differentiation is the process by which meristematic cells lose the ability to divide and take up a permanent shape, size and function to form permanent tissue.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the connective tissue that stores fat and acts as an insulator under the skin.", a: "Adipose tissue.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write two differences between xylem and phloem.", a: "(1) Xylem conducts water and minerals; phloem conducts food prepared in the leaves.\n(2) Xylem conducts only upward; phloem conducts in both directions. (Also: most xylem cells are dead, while phloem cells except fibres are living.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between a ligament and a tendon.", a: "A ligament connects bone to bone; it is very elastic and strong.\nA tendon connects muscle to bone; it is strong but has limited flexibility.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the three types of meristematic tissue and state the location and function of each.", a: "(1) Apical meristem: at the tips of roots and stems; increases length.\n(2) Lateral meristem (cambium): along the sides of stems and roots; increases girth (thickness).\n(3) Intercalary meristem: near the nodes, at the base of leaves or internodes; helps growth in length of those parts.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name three types of epithelial tissue with one location of each.", a: "(1) Squamous epithelium: lining of blood vessels and lung alveoli (stratified in skin).\n(2) Cuboidal epithelium: kidney tubules and ducts of glands.\n(3) Columnar epithelium: inner lining of the intestine (ciliated columnar in the respiratory tract).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is parenchyma? Name its two modified forms and state the function of each.", a: "Parenchyma is a simple permanent tissue of living, thin-walled, loosely packed cells with large intercellular spaces; it stores food.\nChlorenchyma: parenchyma with chlorophyll, which carries out photosynthesis.\nAerenchyma: parenchyma with large air cavities, which helps aquatic plants to float.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Compare striated, smooth and cardiac muscles on the basis of (i) shape of cells, (ii) number of nuclei, (iii) control, (iv) location and (v) one other feature.", a: "Striated: (i) long, cylindrical, unbranched; (ii) multinucleate; (iii) voluntary; (iv) attached to bones, e.g. limbs; (v) shows light and dark bands.\nSmooth: (i) spindle-shaped; (ii) uninucleate; (iii) involuntary; (iv) alimentary canal, blood vessels, iris; (v) no striations.\nCardiac: (i) cylindrical and branched; (ii) uninucleate; (iii) involuntary; (iv) heart; (v) contracts rhythmically throughout life.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Name five types of connective tissue and state one function or feature of each.", a: "1. Blood: fluid matrix (plasma) carrying RBCs, WBCs and platelets; transports gases, food and wastes.\n2. Bone: hard matrix of calcium and phosphorus; forms the framework of the body.\n3. Cartilage: smoothens bone surfaces at joints; found in nose, ear and trachea.\n4. Areolar tissue: fills the spaces inside organs.\n5. Adipose tissue: stores fat and insulates the body.\n(Ligament and tendon are also acceptable.)", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Kiran observes a water lily, a coconut and a leaf stalk. The long stalk of the water lily floats easily on water. The coconut has a tough, fibrous husk. A leaf stalk bends in the wind but does not break.\n(i) Which tissue helps the water lily to float?\n(ii) Which tissue makes the coconut husk hard, and what substance makes its cell walls stiff?\n(iii) Which tissue gives flexibility to the leaf stalk?", a: "(i) Aerenchyma, a parenchyma with large air cavities.\n(ii) Sclerenchyma, whose dead cells have walls thickened with lignin.\n(iii) Collenchyma, whose living cells are thickened at the corners, giving flexibility.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
